WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
<v Maya>Welcome back to Chief of Staff Confidential, the show where we help chiefs of staff get better at the job, one episode at a time.</v>

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.780
<v Maya>I'm Maya, and I'm here with Marcus.</v>

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.220
<v Maya>Marcus, today we're doing something a little different.</v>

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.760
<v Maya>No single topic.</v>

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.000
<v Maya>Instead, I want your reading list.</v>

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.500
<v Maya>If an aspiring chief of staff asked you which books actually matter, what would you hand them?</v>

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.300
<v Marcus>I'd hand them five books.</v>

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.920
<v Marcus>And I won't just name them.</v>

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.680
<v Marcus>I'll give you the one takeaway from each that a chief of staff can use on Monday morning.</v>

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.400
<v Marcus>Let's start with Extreme Ownership, by Jocko Willink and Leif Babin.</v>

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.680
<v Marcus>Two Navy SEAL officers, and the core idea is brutally simple.</v>

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.780
<v Marcus>When anything goes wrong, the leader owns it.</v>

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:43.760
<v Marcus>All of it.</v>

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.660
<v Marcus>No blaming the team, no blaming circumstances, no blaming the fog of war.</v>

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.080
<v Maya>That sounds intense for an office job.</v>

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:56.000
<v Maya>And a chief of staff usually doesn't even have formal authority over most of the people involved.</v>

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.500
<v Maya>How does that apply?</v>

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.800
<v Marcus>That's exactly why it matters more, not less.</v>

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.760
<v Marcus>A chief of staff carries enormous responsibility with very little formal power.</v>

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:14.220
<v Marcus>When a launch slips, or a briefing doc isn't ready, or a meeting goes sideways, the easiest thing in the world is to point somewhere else.</v>

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:21.260
<v Marcus>Extreme ownership means you say, that happened on my watch, and here's what I'm changing so it doesn't happen again.</v>

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.400
<v Marcus>Principals notice that fast.</v>

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.980
<v Marcus>It builds trust faster than almost anything else you can do.</v>

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:32.020
<v Maya>So book one is really about what you do in the worst moment, not the best one.</v>

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:32.760
<v Marcus>Exactly.</v>

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:34.540
<v Marcus>Anyone can take credit.</v>

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.000
<v Marcus>Ownership is proven when things break.</v>

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.460
<v Maya>Okay, I'm sold.</v>

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:39.540
<v Maya>What's number two?</v>

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.800
<v Marcus>Number two is Multipliers, by Liz Wiseman.</v>

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.400
<v Marcus>She studied leaders across industries and found two types.</v>

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.780
<v Marcus>Diminishers drain the intelligence out of a room.</v>

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.700
<v Marcus>They always need to be the smartest person there, so everyone else gets smaller.</v>

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.900
<v Marcus>Multipliers do the opposite.</v>

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.380
<v Marcus>They amplify everyone around them.</v>

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.620
<v Marcus>They ask the questions that make other people smarter.</v>

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:10.320
<v Maya>That framing feels perfect for this role, because a chief of staff's entire job is making the principal and the team more effective.</v>

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.940
<v Marcus>That's why it's on my list.</v>

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.560
<v Marcus>The takeaway is a question you ask yourself at the end of every day.</v>

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.260
<v Marcus>Did I multiply or diminish today?</v>

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:24.600
<v Marcus>Did I make my principal sharper and my team stronger, or did I just make myself look useful?</v>

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.740
<v Marcus>Here's a practical version.</v>

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:33.720
<v Marcus>In your next meeting, instead of giving your opinion first, ask the quietest person in the room what they think.</v>

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:35.780
<v Marcus>That's a multiplier move.</v>

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.080
<v Marcus>It costs you nothing and it changes the room.</v>

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:40.280
<v Maya>I like that.</v>

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.560
<v Maya>A daily question and one small habit.</v>

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:43.620
<v Maya>Book three?</v>

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.380
<v Marcus>Book three is The First 90 Days, by Michael Watkins.</v>

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.320
<v Marcus>This is for anyone stepping into the chief of staff role, or any big new role.</v>

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.820
<v Marcus>Watkins' point is that transitions are when leaders fail most often, and it's rarely about competence.</v>

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.960
<v Marcus>It's about not having a plan for the transition itself.</v>

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:10.280
<v Marcus>He lays out how to learn fast, secure early wins, and build the relationships that matter before you actually need them.</v>

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.140
<v Maya>So it's not, show up and be brilliant on day one.</v>

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:14.860
<v Marcus>Right.</v>

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.440
<v Marcus>It's, have a plan for day one through day ninety.</v>

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.600
<v Marcus>Your first impression compounds, for better or worse.</v>

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.460
<v Marcus>The chiefs of staff who struggle most are the ones who try to prove themselves before they understand the terrain.</v>

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:31.260
<v Marcus>They reorganize the operating cadence in week two and wonder why everyone's cold.</v>

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.360
<v Marcus>Watkins gives you the sequence.</v>

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.500
<v Marcus>Learn first, align second, then act.</v>

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.900
<v Marcus>And pick one or two early wins that matter to your principal, not to your ego.</v>

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.000
<v Maya>Learn, align, act.</v>

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.580
<v Maya>That's going on a sticky note.</v>

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.100
<v Maya>What about the day to day grind, once you're settled in the seat?</v>

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.520
<v Marcus>That's book four.</v>

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.540
<v Marcus>High Output Management, by Andy Grove, the legendary Intel CEO.</v>

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.920
<v Marcus>Grove's big idea is leverage.</v>

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:02.960
<v Marcus>Every activity you do has leverage, meaning how much output it creates across the whole organization.</v>

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.860
<v Marcus>A one on one with a struggling manager feels small in the moment.</v>

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.060
<v Marcus>But if that conversation unblocks a team of twenty people, that's enormous leverage.</v>

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.420
<v Marcus>Compare that to perfecting a slide deck that three people will glance at.</v>

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.860
<v Marcus>A chief of staff lives in leverage.</v>

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:20.700
<v Marcus>Your calendar is the job.</v>

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.520
<v Marcus>Grove teaches you to audit your week and move your hours toward the highest leverage work.</v>

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.820
<v Maya>I think a lot of chiefs of staff feel guilty about how much of their week disappears into meetings and one on ones.</v>

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.320
<v Maya>Grove would say that is the work.</v>

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.620
<v Marcus>He would say exactly that.</v>

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.780
<v Marcus>Managerial leverage is the work.</v>

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.840
<v Marcus>The guilt comes from measuring yourself like an individual contributor.</v>

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:44.140
<v Marcus>You're not one.</v>

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.580
<v Marcus>Your output is the output of the people around you.</v>

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.440
<v Maya>That's a reframe worth the price of the book alone.</v>

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:51.980
<v Maya>And the last one?</v>

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.240
<v Marcus>Book five is Essentialism, by Greg McKeown.</v>

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.340
<v Marcus>The disciplined pursuit of less.</v>

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.620
<v Marcus>The takeaway for a chief of staff is about protecting the principal's time and your own.</v>

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.040
<v Marcus>Every yes you give is a thousand future nos you don't get to say.</v>

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.820
<v Marcus>The best chiefs of staff are ruthless, polite gatekeepers.</v>

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.700
<v Marcus>Essentialism gives you the philosophy and the language for it.</v>

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.200
<v Marcus>If it isn't a clear yes, it's a no.</v>

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:19.020
<v Marcus>And here's the practical part.</v>

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.220
<v Marcus>You don't say no by slamming the door.</v>

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.240
<v Marcus>You say, here's what we'd have to drop to make room for that, is that the trade you want?</v>

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.680
<v Marcus>Suddenly you're not the person who says no.</v>

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.120
<v Marcus>You're the person who makes tradeoffs visible.</v>

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.480
<v Maya>Okay, let me recap the shelf.</v>

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.240
<v Maya>Extreme Ownership: own everything, especially when things break.</v>

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:45.300
<v Maya>Multipliers: amplify everyone around you, and ask yourself daily whether you multiplied or diminished.</v>

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:51.220
<v Maya>The First 90 Days: plan your transition, learn, align, then act.</v>

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:57.580
<v Maya>High Output Management: your calendar is the job, spend your hours where the leverage is highest.</v>

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:05.000
<v Maya>And Essentialism: less but better, protect the calendar, and make tradeoffs visible instead of just saying no.</v>

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.080
<v Marcus>That's the list.</v>

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:07.920
<v Marcus>Five books, five habits.</v>

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.920
<v Marcus>Read them in any order, but read them with a pen.</v>

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.720
<v Marcus>The margin notes are where the real value is.</v>

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.640
<v Marcus>And honestly, reread one of them every year.</v>

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:21.580
<v Marcus>You'll be a different chief of staff each time, so you'll read a different book each time.</v>

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.100
<v Maya>Great place to end.</v>

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.100
<v Maya>That's our episode, the reading list.</v>

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:30.680
<v Maya>If you pick up even one of these this month, we'd love to hear how it lands.</v>

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.980
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:37.320
<v Maya>See you tomorrow.</v>

